<blockquote>"Chabuti Royal Pudding.”<br />By infusing sweetened, lemon-soured, long neck cream with nut-vittle for several days until yellowed, it is possible then to slide the set pudding over a baked biscuit. From here it can be served hot or cold. Chef Molin always serves hot, with a scatter of fresh seasonal herbs to cut through the richness.</blockquote>

<blockquote>"Wine Snecket."<br />A portfolio of fresh creamy cheeses verging on over-ripeness, as is the custom of this region, are "jacketed" or "suited" in a small amount of the wine sauce. Wine Snecket needs to be sharp and strong with wine, and as such, it is not a dish for wee tots. The dish is normally served with sweet fresh berries and a crisp-grain tuile.</blockquote>

<blockquote>"Grapes with white custard."<br />This time it is not just sweetness, but the comforting blanket of white custard that neutralises the gooey grapes that are in turns dried and dunked in water until they resemble gigantic sultanas. The dish is traditionally served with a tall cylindrical sponge to give it height.</blockquote>

